 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Watchdog cycle",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Each valid level change on WDI, in either direction, restarts the internal timer. WDO stays high while edges keep arriving within tWD. When tWD passes with no edge, WDO is driven low for tWDO; when it returns high, the timeout count starts again from zero."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Startup delay",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "At power-up, and whenever a SET input changes, the part first waits an internal setup time of up to 300 µs, then runs a startup delay tDELAY. The timeout count does not run and WDO does not pulse until the startup delay ends. To avoid a spurious pulse, WDI toggling should begin before the shortest startup delay has run out and should then repeat faster than the shortest timeout."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Timing per device",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "MAX6369/MAX6370: startup delay is the same as the timeout, pin-set from 1 ms to 60 s (minimum values). MAX6371/MAX6372: startup delay fixed at 60 s, timeout pin-set from 1 ms to 60 s. MAX6373/MAX6374: five settings give startup delays of 200 µs to 60 s and timeouts of 3 ms to 10 s; two settings (101 and 110) hold off the timer until the first WDI edge, with no limit on how long that takes, for systems needing more than 60 s to start."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input timing",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "A WDI edge counts only if it belongs to a pulse at least 100 ns wide; timing runs from the last such edge. WDI is ignored while WDO is low, during the startup delay, and during the setup time of up to 300 µs that follows power-up or any SET change."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Changing the SET inputs",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "SET0-SET2 may be strapped or driven by logic and changed in operation. Toggling WDI just before a change lowers the chance that the change lands late in a countdown and causes an unwanted pulse. With WDO low at the moment of change, the pulse in progress runs to its end before the new code takes over; with WDO high, the new code takes over at once. Either way a fresh startup delay follows the 300 µs setup interval."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Disable",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Code 011 (SET2 = 0, SET1 = 1, SET0 = 1) turns the watchdog off on every device; WDO stays high. Moving to any other code turns it back on without cycling power; once 300 µs of setup have passed, a fresh startup delay begins."
  },
  {
   "topic": "First-edge mode",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "On MAX6373/MAX6374, codes 101 and 110 end the startup phase on the first WDI transition instead of on a timer. To enter first-edge mode while running, select disable (011) first and then the first-edge code; this keeps WDO deasserted and prevents WDI from being masked."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "MAX6369/71/73 have an active-low open-drain WDO that sinks current while asserted, with a 100 ms pulse; pull it up to any supply up to +5.5 V. The pull-up must be large enough for a valid low (see VOL) and small enough to give a valid high while feeding all input and leakage currents on the line; 10 kΩ suits most uses. MAX6370/72/74 have a push-pull active-low WDO with a 1 ms pulse."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Noisy environments",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "For use amid heavy electrical noise, a 0.1 µF capacitor goes from VCC to GND, placed as close to the part as can be and within 0.2 in of it."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Software strategy",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Rather than pulsing WDI high-low-high from one place, drive it high at one point in the program and low at another: for example high at the top of the main loop, low as each subroutine or loop finishes. A program stuck in a loop then stops producing edges, the timer expires and WDO pulses."
  }
 ]
